The baseline does one plaintext multiply with a rescale, plus one plaintext add (which needs encoding). The cost is dominated by the ct-pt multiply (~7 ms) and its rescale, and by the plaintext encode for B.

The function y = x*A + B needs one multiplication by a vector, so it cannot avoid that multiply. The options are to change the circuit's shape:

- Adding B before the multiply would need B/A, which is plaintext-plaintext arithmetic and not allowed.
- Since B is added at the post-multiply scale, the plain add must encode B at that scale. That encoding is unavoidable.

One alternative is to reorder the operations as (x + B') * A, but B' = B/A cannot be computed. So the circuit stays essentially the same. I will try a variant that differs structurally: compute x*A as a ciphertext product and add B via a subtraction of a negated term. That is equivalent after normalization and is unlikely to help, but it is the only variation available without changing the math.

Nothing clearly cheaper exists, so I resubmit the baseline form, which is already minimal.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The circuit already has one plaintext multiply, one rescale and one plaintext add, and I see no way to remove any of them. B/A would be plaintext-plaintext arithmetic, which is not allowed. This attempt adds B by subtracting a negated plaintext instead. It might change how CHEHAB handles the encoding of B and the scale alignment. If plaintext negation is rejected, the next attempt should go back to the baseline form.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = x * a;
  Ciphertext nt = -t;
  Ciphertext y = -(nt - b);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

This attempt tries a different form: negate the plaintext B directly and subtract it. That avoids the two extra ciphertext negations of the last attempt, which added adds and depth. The multiply and rescale are unchanged. If CHEHAB rejects negating a plaintext, I will return to the baseline form.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = x * a;
  Ciphertext y = t - (-b);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I can't find a cheaper circuit. The multiply, rescale and plaintext add are all required, and B/A is not allowed. I'll try one more structural variant: multiply the negated ciphertext by A, then subtract that product from B's contribution. It computes the same function but may change how CHEHAB aligns scales.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext nx = -x;
  Ciphertext t = nx * a;
  Ciphertext y = b - t;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I haven't found a circuit cheaper than the baseline. It already has the minimum: one plaintext multiply with its rescale, and one plaintext add. Every alternative I tried either normalized to the same circuit or added operations. I'm resubmitting the baseline form.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I have no structurally different circuit that is cheaper. The function needs one plaintext multiply, which forces one rescale, and one plaintext add. The only other forms (negations, reordering, splitting the multiply) add operations or normalize back to the baseline. I'm keeping the baseline form, which is the minimal circuit.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a;
  y += b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I still have no cheaper circuit. Every form I tried either normalizes to the baseline circuit or adds operations (attempt 2 added two adds and was slower at 19.31 ms).

The floor is one plaintext multiply with its rescale, plus one plaintext add. Folding B into the multiply would need B/A or a ciphertext of ones, and CKKS rules 1 and 2 forbid both. The baseline form is therefore the best available, and I'm resubmitting it unchanged.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a + b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I have no new idea for a cheaper circuit in this last attempt. The baseline circuit has the minimum operation set (one plaintext multiply, one rescale, one plaintext add), and each variant I tried normalized to it or added operations. I'm leaving the baseline form as my answer.

